---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import BarSet from '../components/BarSet.astro';
import Facts from '../components/Facts.astro';
import { BALANCE, YEAR } from '../data/books.js';
import { NET_POSITION_SPLIT, APPROPRIATIONS, RESERVES } from '../data/pages.js';
---

<Base
  title="The Books — Oklahoma Inc."
  description="What Oklahoma owns, what it owes, what came in and what went out. Taken from the state's audited annual report for the year ended June 30, 2024."
  sources={['acfr','budget']}
  notes={[
    { title: 'On these statements', text: '“Oklahoma Inc.” presents public data in the shape of corporate financial statements for clarity. The State is not a company and these are interpretive constructs rather than GAAP filings.' },
    { title: 'On the shared scale', text: 'Both sets of bars are drawn against the same maximum, so their lengths can be compared directly against one another rather than only within their own group.' },
  ]}
  image="/books.jpg"
  imageAlt="Ledgers and record keeping"
>
  <section class="panel panel--tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1 class="mega">What the state<br />owns and owes.</h1>
      <p class="under">
        Government-wide statement of net position, June 30, 2024. Against $46.29 billion
        of assets sit $12.93 billion of liabilities, leaving $34.08 billion. By state
        standards that is a strong balance sheet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">The two statements</h2>

      <div class="ledgers">
        <BarSet caption={BALANCE.caption} rows={BALANCE.rows} ref="acfr" />
        <BarSet
          caption={YEAR.caption}
          rows={YEAR.rows}
          ref="acfr"
          scale={Math.max(...BALANCE.rows.map((r) => r.value))}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="prose pad">
        <p>
          Revenues of <strong>$33.29 billion</strong> exceeded expenses of
          <strong>$31.45 billion</strong>, producing a <strong>$1.84 billion</strong>
          surplus and a 5.9% increase in the net position. A $500 million fall in
          gross-production tax, reflecting commodity price movement, was offset by growth
          in income tax, sales tax and federal grants. Federal grants totalled
          $14.49 billion, or 43.5% of all revenue.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--deep">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">Not all of it<br />can be spent.</h2>

      <div class="pad">
        <BarSet
          caption="Composition of the $34.08 billion net position"
          rows={NET_POSITION_SPLIT}
          ref="acfr"
          scale={34.08}
          deep={true}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="prose pad">
        <p>
          Only the third of these is genuinely free. <strong>$13.4 billion</strong> is tied
          up in capital assets the state cannot sell — the roads it drives on.
          <strong>$9.2 billion</strong> is legally restricted to specific uses. That leaves
          <strong>$11.4 billion</strong> of real flexibility.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="pad"><Facts caption="Reserves and fund balances" rows={RESERVES} /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">$12.08 billion<br />of it is a choice.</h2>
      <p class="under">
        The audited statements count every federal dollar and every fee. The appropriated
        budget is the part the legislature directly decides. In real terms per person it is
        about 11.8% below the FY2000 level.
      </p>

      <div class="pad">
        <BarSet
          caption="FY2026 appropriations by area"
          rows={APPROPRIATIONS}
          ref="budget"
          scale={5.76}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .ledgers {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(24rem, 100%), 1fr));
    gap: var(--s-8);
  }
</style>
